<description>
Connect each same-color pair of marker pixels with a straight segment, drawing vertical segments over horizontal ones.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- Each foreground color appears in exactly two marker pixels.
- The two markers of each color share exactly one row or exactly one column.

Rule
- Draw the same-color horizontal or vertical segment between each marker pair, inclusive.
- Paint horizontal segments first, then vertical segments so crossings keep the vertical segment color.

Key insight
- The colored pixels are not isolated objects; same-color pairs are endpoints of axis-aligned segments, and segment orientation determines the crossing layer.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "same-color foreground marker pixel pairs on the background",
  "path": "straight row or column segment between the two markers of each color, inclusive",
  "trajectory": "paint every pixel on each segment with the marker-pair color",
  "transform": "keep all other background pixels unchanged",
  "multi_step": "draw horizontal segments first, then vertical segments so vertical-horizontal crossings take the vertical color"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 20-30 rows x 10-20 cols, background 0
  markers:
    count: 5 distinct non-zero colors, each appearing as exactly 2 pixels
    alignment: each pair shares exactly one row (horizontal) or one column (vertical)
    mix: 2-3 horizontal pairs + 2-3 vertical pairs (total 5) [observed]
  segments:
    length: 4-18 cells (endpoints inclusive)
    no_same_orientation_overlap: horizontal segments share no cells; vertical segments share no cells
    crossings: 2-3 H-V crossings per grid [observed]; some segments may cross 0 others
  output:
    rule: fill each segment between its two same-color endpoints with that color
    layer_order: draw horizontal segments first, then vertical; at crossings vertical color wins
    unchanged: all non-segment background pixels stay 0

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first, then strip filled segments back to endpoint-only input
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size H in [20,30], W in [10,20]
    2. Choose 5 distinct non-zero colors (from 1-9)
    3. Assign each color as horizontal or vertical (2-3 of each)
    4. For each horizontal color, pick a row and two distinct columns (gap >= 3) as endpoints
    5. For each vertical color, pick a column and two distinct rows (gap >= 3) as endpoints
    6. Validate no two horizontal segments share any cell, no two vertical segments share any cell
    7. Ensure at least 2 H-V crossings exist (retry placement if not)
    8. Build output by drawing all horizontal segments then vertical segments (V overwrites at crossings)
    9. Build input by placing only the 2 endpoint pixels per color on a blank grid
```
